					<div id=3D"mw-content-text" class=3D"mw-body-content"><div class=3D"mw=
-subjectpageheader">
</div><div class=3D"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr=
"><div class=3D"shortdescription nomobile noexcerpt noprint searchaux" styl=
e=3D"display:none">Artificial intelligence model paradigm</div>
<p class=3D"mw-empty-elt">
</p><p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence"=
 title=3D"Artificial intelligence">artificial intelligence</a>, a <b>founda=
tion model</b> (<b>FM</b>), also known as <b>large <i>x</i> model</b> (<b>L=
xM</b>, where "x" is a variable representing any text, image, sound, etc.),=
 is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning" title=3D"M=
achine learning">machine learning</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Deep_learning" title=3D"Deep learning">deep learning</a> model train=
ed on vast datasets so that it can be applied across a wide range of use ca=
ses.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:1_1-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:1-1"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_artificial_intelligence" class=3D"mw=
-redirect" title=3D"Generative artificial intelligence">Generative AI</a> a=
pplications like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_mo=
del" title=3D"Large language model">large language models</a> (LLM) are com=
mon examples of foundation models.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:1_1-1" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-=
:1-1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup>
</p><p>Building foundation models is often highly resource-intensive, with =
the most advanced models costing hundreds of millions of dollars to cover t=
he expenses of acquiring, curating, and processing massive datasets, as wel=
l as the compute power required for training.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-2" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-2"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup> These costs stem from the need for sophisticated infr=
astructure, extended training times, and advanced hardware, such as <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPU" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"GPU">=
GPUs</a>. In contrast, adapting an existing foundation model for a specific=
 task or using it directly is far less costly, as it leverages pre-trained =
capabilities and typically requires only fine-tuning on smaller, task-speci=
fic datasets.
</p><p>Early examples of foundation models are <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Language_models" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Language model=
s">language models</a> like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genera=
tive_pre-trained_transformer" title=3D"Generative pre-trained transformer">=
OpenAI's GPT</a> series and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google=
" title=3D"Google">Google</a>'s <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BE=
RT_(language_model)" title=3D"BERT (language model)">BERT</a>.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foun=
dation_model#cite_note-3"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-4" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-4">=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>4<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> Beyond text, foundation models have been developed across a rang=
e of modalities=E2=80=94including <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
DALL-E" title=3D"DALL-E">DALL-E</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Stable_diffusion" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Stable diffusion">Stabl=
e diffusion</a>, and Flamingo<sup id=3D"cite_ref-deepmind_20220428_5-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#=
cite_note-deepmind_20220428-5"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>5<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> for images, MusicGen<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-6" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foun=
dation_model#cite_note-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and LLark<sup id=3D"cite_ref-7" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-7"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>7<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup> for music, and RT-2<sup id=3D"cite_ref-8" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_not=
e-8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> for robotic control. Foundation models are also being devel=
oped for fields like astronomy,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-9" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-9"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>9<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> radiology,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-10" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-10"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=20
ophthalmology,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-11" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-11"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>11<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
genomics,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-12" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-12"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>12<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> coding,<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-13" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Foundation_model#cite_note-13"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>13<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Time_series" title=3D"Time series">time series</a> forecasting=
,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-14" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-14"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>14<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> mathematics,<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-15" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Foundation_model#cite_note-15"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and chemistry.<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-16" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Found=
ation_model#cite_note-16"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>16<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<meta property=3D"mw:PageProp/toc">
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Definitions">Definitions</h=
2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</=
span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_mod=
el&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" title=3D"Edit section: Definitions"><=
span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span><=
/div>
<p>The Stanford Institute for Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence's (HAI=
) Center for Research on Foundation Models (CRFM) coined the term "foundati=
on model" in August 2021<sup id=3D"cite_ref-CRFM_17-0" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-CRFM-17=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>17<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup> to mean "any model that is trained on broad data (generally u=
sing <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-supervised_learning" tit=
le=3D"Self-supervised learning">self-supervision</a> at scale) that can be =
adapted (e.g., fine-tuned) to a wide range of downstream tasks".<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-Bommasani_20210818_18-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-Bommasani_20210818-18"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>18<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup> This was based on their observation that preexisting terms, while ov=
erlapping, were not adequate, stating that "'<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Large_language_model" title=3D"Large language model">large langu=
age model</a>' was too narrow given the focus is not only language; 'self-s=
upervised model' was too specific to the training objective; and 'pretraine=
d model' suggested that the noteworthy action all happened after 'pretraini=
ng."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-19" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-19"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>19<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The term "foundati=
on model" was chosen over "foundational model"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-20" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-20"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>20<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> because "foundational" implies that these models pr=
ovide fundamental principles in a way that "foundation" does not.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-21" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Foundation_model#cite_note-21"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>21<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The term <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Vision-language_model" title=3D"Vision-language model">vi=
sion-language model</a> (VLM) is also used as a near-synonym.
</p><p>As governments regulate foundation models, new legal definitions hav=
e emerged.
</p>
<ul><li>In the United States, the now revoked <i><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_14110" title=3D"Executive Order 14110">Execu=
tive Order on the Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Development and Use of Arti=
ficial Intelligence</a></i> defined a foundation model as "an AI model that=
 is trained on broad data; generally uses <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Self-supervised_learning" title=3D"Self-supervised learning">self-s=
upervision</a>; contains at least tens of billions of parameters; is applic=
able across a wide range of contexts".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-22" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-=
22"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>22<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup></li>
<li>In the United States, the proposed AI Foundation Model Transparency Act=
 of 2023<sup id=3D"cite_ref-23" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-23"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>23<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> by House Repre=
sentatives <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Beyer" title=3D"Don=
 Beyer">Don Beyer</a> (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_=
Party_(United_States)" title=3D"Democratic Party (United States)">D</a>=E2=
=80=93<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia" title=3D"Virginia"=
>VA</a>) and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Eshoo" title=3D"=
Anna Eshoo">Anna Eshoo</a> (D, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cal=
ifornia" title=3D"California">CA</a>) defines a foundation model as "an art=
ificial intelligence model trained on broad data, generally uses self super=
vision, generally contains at least 1,000,000,000 parameters, is applicable=
 across a wide range of contexts, and exhibits, or could be easily modified=
 to exhibit, high levels of performance at tasks that could pose a serious =
risk to security, national economic security, national public health or saf=
ety, or any combination of those matters."</li>
<li>In the European Union, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eur=
opean_Parliament" title=3D"European Parliament">European Parliament</a>'s n=
egotiated position on the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_Intelligence_Act" title=3D"Artificial Intelligence Act">E.U. AI Act</a> =
defines a foundation model as an "AI model that is trained on broad data at=
 scale, is designed for generality of output, and can be adapted to a wide =
range of distinctive tasks".</li>
<li>In the United Kingdom, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Com=
petition_and_Markets_Authority" title=3D"Competition and Markets Authority"=
>Competition and Markets Authority</a>'s <i>AI Foundation Models: Initial R=
eport</i><sup id=3D"cite_ref-:1_1-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:1-1"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> defines =
foundation models as "a type of AI technology that are trained on vast amou=
nts of data that can be adapted to a wide range of tasks and operations."</=
li></ul>
<p>The United States's definitions are the only ones to make reference to t=
he size of a foundation model, and differ on magnitude. Beyer and Eshoo's d=
efinition also specifies that foundation models must achieve a level of per=
formance as to be a potential danger. In contrast, the E.U. definition requ=
ires the model to be designed for generality of output. All definitions agr=
ee that foundation models must be trained on a broad range of data with pot=
ential applications in many domains.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"History">History</h2><span =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp;a=
ction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Edit section: History"><span>edit</s=
pan></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Technologically, foundation models are built using established machine l=
earning techniques like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_neura=
l_networks" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Deep neural networks">deep neura=
l networks</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfer_learning"=
 title=3D"Transfer learning">transfer learning</a>, and <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-supervised_learning" title=3D"Self-supervised le=
arning">self-supervised learning</a>. Foundation models differ from previou=
s techniques as they are general purpose models that function as a reusable=
 infrastructure, instead of bespoke and one-off task-specific models.
</p><p>Advances in computer parallelism (e.g., <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/CUDA" title=3D"CUDA">CUDA GPUs</a>) and new developments in ne=
ural network architecture (e.g., <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T=
ransformer_(machine_learning_model)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Transfo=
rmer (machine learning model)">Transformers</a>), and the increased use of =
training data with minimal supervision all contributed to the rise of found=
ation models. Foundation models began to materialize as the latest wave of =
deep learning models in the late 2010s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:2_24-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-:2-24"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>24<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup> Relative to most prior work on deep learning, th=
ese language models demonstrated the potential of training on much larger w=
eb-sourced datasets using self-supervised objectives (e.g. predicting the n=
ext word in a large corpus of text). These approaches, which draw upon earl=
ier works like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word2vec" title=3D"=
Word2vec">word2vec</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GloVe" =
title=3D"GloVe">GloVe</a>, deviated from prior supervised approaches that r=
equired annotated data (e.g. crowd-sourced labels).
</p><p>The 2022 releases of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stable=
_Diffusion" title=3D"Stable Diffusion">Stable Diffusion</a> and <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChatGPT" title=3D"ChatGPT">ChatGPT</a> (initi=
ally powered by the GPT-3.5 model) led to foundation models and generative =
AI entering widespread public discourse. Further, releases of <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LLaMA" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"LLaMA">LL=
aMA</a>, Llama 2, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mistral_AI" =
title=3D"Mistral AI">Mistral</a> in 2023 contributed to a greater emphasis =
placed on how foundation models are released with open foundation models ga=
rnering a lot of support<sup id=3D"cite_ref-25" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-25"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>25<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> and scrutiny.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-26" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-26"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>26<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Related_concepts">Related c=
oncepts</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoun=
dation_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3" title=3D"Edit section: Rela=
ted concepts"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]=
</span></span></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Frontier_models">Frontier m=
odels</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-brac=
ket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFounda=
tion_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D4" title=3D"Edit section: Fronti=
er models"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</s=
pan></span></div>
<p>Certain highly advanced foundation models are termed "frontier models", =
which have the potential to "possess dangerous capabilities sufficient to p=
ose severe risks to public safety."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:0_27-0" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_not=
e-:0-27"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>27<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> These "dangerous capabilities" stem from the accidenta=
l or intentional misuse of such models, which in conjunction with their pow=
erful nature can lead to severe harms. As foundation models continue to imp=
rove, some AI researchers speculate that almost all next-generation foundat=
ion models will be considered frontier models.
</p><p>Since the concept of dangerous capabilities is inherently subjective=
, there is no strict designation for what foundation models qualify as fron=
tier models. However, some generally held ideas for sufficiently dangerous =
capabilities include:
</p>
<ul><li>Designing and synthesizing new biological or chemical weapons<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-28" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Foundation_model#cite_note-28"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>28<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li>Producing and propagating convincing, tailored disinformation with mini=
mal user instruction<sup id=3D"cite_ref-29" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-29"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>29<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></l=
i>
<li>Harnessing unprecedented offensive cyber capabilities<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-30" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundati=
on_model#cite_note-30"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>30<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li>Evading human control through deceptive means<sup id=3D"cite_ref-31" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model=
#cite_note-31"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>31<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup></li></ul>
<p>The U.S. definition (via the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence_safety_institute" title=3D"Artificial intelligence sa=
fety institute">AI Safety Institute</a> at <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/National_Institute_of_Standards_and_Technology" title=3D"National =
Institute of Standards and Technology">NIST</a>) tied "frontier" status als=
o to the compute technical threshold to create objective legal requirements=
.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-32" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-32"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>32<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=20
</p><p>Due to frontier models' unique capabilities, it is difficult to effe=
ctively regulate their development and deployment. Because of their emergen=
t nature, new dangerous capabilities can appear on their own in frontier mo=
dels, both in the development stage and after being deployed.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-:0_27-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Foundation_model#cite_note-:0-27"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>27<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Additionally, since frontier=
 models continue to adapt after deployment, it remains difficult to mitigat=
e all harms that arise from already-deployed models. If a frontier model ha=
ppens to be open-source or is released online, the model can also dissemina=
te rapidly, further hampering regulators by creating a lack of accountabili=
ty.
</p><p>In response, groups such as the Frontier Model Forum, founded by Ope=
nAI, Anthropic, Google and Microsoft, were created to create safety standar=
ds for these advanced foundational models.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-33" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_n=
ote-33"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>33<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup> A global network of governmental and intergovernmental =
bodies has emerged to create formal safety standards, including the Global =
Network of AI Safety Institutes (AISIs)<sup id=3D"cite_ref-34" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note=
-34"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>34<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> and the G7"Hiroshima AI Process" (HAIP)<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-35" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundatio=
n_model#cite_note-35"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>35<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"General-purpose_AI">General=
-purpose AI</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsectio=
n-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3D=
Foundation_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5" title=3D"Edit section: =
General-purpose AI"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-brac=
ket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Due to their adaptability to a wide range of use-cases, foundation model=
s are sometimes considered to be examples of general-purpose AI. In designi=
ng the EU AI Act, the European Parliament has stated that a new wave of gen=
eral-purpose AI technologies shapes the overall AI ecosystem.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-36" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foun=
dation_model#cite_note-36"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>36<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The fuller structure of the ecosyste=
m, in addition to the properties of specific general-purpose AI systems, in=
fluences the design of AI policy and research.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-37" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-37"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>37<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> General-purpose AI systems also often appear in peo=
ple's everyday lives through applications and tools like <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChatGPT" title=3D"ChatGPT">ChatGPT</a> or <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DALL-E" title=3D"DALL-E">DALL-E</a>.
</p><p>Government agencies like EU Parliament have identified regulation of=
 general-purpose AI, such as foundation models, to be a high priority. Gene=
ral-purpose AI systems are often characterized by large size, opacity, and =
potential for emergence, all of which can create unintended harms. Such sys=
tems also heavily influence downstream applications, which further exacerba=
tes the need for regulation. In regards to prominent legislation, a number =
of stakeholders have pushed for the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Artificial_Intelligence_Act" title=3D"Artificial Intelligence Act">EU AI =
Act</a> to include restrictions on general-purpose AI systems, all of which=
 would also apply to foundation models.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"World_models">World models<=
/h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_m=
odel&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6" title=3D"Edit section: World models=
"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></spa=
n></div>
<div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">Main article=
: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_model_(artificial_intellig=
ence)" title=3D"World model (artificial intelligence)">World model (artific=
ial intelligence)</a></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1353705441"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_twin" title=3D"D=
igital twin">Digital twin</a></div>
<p>World models are sometimes described as foundation models.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-38" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foun=
dation_model#cite_note-38"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>38<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-:9_39-0" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_=
note-:9-39"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>39<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> World models are a representation of an environment=
 intended to predict the state of that environment after taking a set of ac=
tions,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-40" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-40"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>40<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-:10_41-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F=
oundation_model#cite_note-:10-41"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>41<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> as well as to implicitly mode=
l physical concepts such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravit=
y" title=3D"Gravity">gravity</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:10_41-1" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note=
-:10-41"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>41<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prompt_engine=
ering" title=3D"Prompt engineering">Input prompts</a> for world models can =
include text or images,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-42" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-42"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>42<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
><sup id=3D"cite_ref-43" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-43"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>43<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> as well as videos or =
3D scenes,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:16_44-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:16-44"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>44<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> an=
d the resulting 3D environments can be exported.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:16_44-=
1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_=
model#cite_note-:16-44"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>44<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> World models, alongside <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embodied_agent" title=3D"Embodied agent">embodie=
d AI</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-agent_system" titl=
e=3D"Multi-agent system">multi-agent models</a>, and neuroscience models of=
 the brain, are seen as alternatives to large language models for achieving=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_general_intelligence" =
title=3D"Artificial general intelligence">general artificial intelligence</=
a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:12_45-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:12-45"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>45<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>World models do not have a fully agreed definition, but have been di=
vided into two scopes: one for representing and understanding the current e=
nvironment, and another for predicting the future state of that environment=
. In the former view, world models are developed using model-based <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_learning" title=3D"Reinforc=
ement learning">reinforcement learning</a> and a <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Markov_decision_process" title=3D"Markov decision process">M=
arkov decision process</a>, using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Model_predictive_control" title=3D"Model predictive control">model predicti=
ve control</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Carlo_tree=
_search" title=3D"Monte Carlo tree search">Monte Carlo tree search</a> to c=
reate policies. With the latter, (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Multimodal_learning" title=3D"Multimodal learning">multimodal</a>) large la=
nguage models or video generation models can be used. In addition, these en=
vironments can be immersive simulations for training AI agents that can int=
eract in the real world.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:13_46-0" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:13-46">=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>46<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"History_2">History</h4><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp=
;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7" title=3D"Edit section: History"><span>edit<=
/span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p><i>Quanta Magazine</i> traced world models back to a 1943 publication by=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenneth_Craik" title=3D"Kenneth C=
raik">Kenneth Craik</a> on mental models and the <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Blocks_world" title=3D"Blocks world">blocks world</a> of <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHRDLU" title=3D"SHRDLU">SHRDLU</a> i=
n the 1960s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-47" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-47"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>47<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <i>Busines=
s Insider</i> traced world models to a 1971 paper by <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Jay_Wright_Forrester" title=3D"Jay Wright Forrester">Jay=
 Wright Forrester</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:12_45-1" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:12-45"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>45<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> A related idea of organizing world knowledge, the <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frame_(artificial_intelligence)" title=3D"Frame (=
artificial intelligence)">frame representation</a>, was proposed by <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvin_Minsky" title=3D"Marvin Minsky">Ma=
rvin Minsky</a> in 1974.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:13_46-1" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:13-46">=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>46<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup>
</p><p>In 2018, researchers David Ha and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/J%C3%BCrgen_Schmidhuber" title=3D"J=C3=BCrgen Schmidhuber">J=C3=BCrg=
en Schmidhuber</a> defined world models in the context of <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_learning" title=3D"Reinforcement lear=
ning">reinforcement learning</a>: an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Intelligent_agent" title=3D"Intelligent agent">agent</a> with a <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variational_autoencoder" title=3D"Variati=
onal autoencoder">variational autoencoder</a> model <b>V</b> for representi=
ng visual observations, a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurren=
t_neural_network" title=3D"Recurrent neural network">recurrent neural netwo=
rk</a> model <b>M</b> for representing memory, and a <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_model" title=3D"Linear model">linear model</a> <b=
>C</b> for making decisions. They suggested that agents trained on world mo=
dels in environments that simulate reality could be applied to real world s=
ettings.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-48" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-48"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>48<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In 2022, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yann_LeCun" title=
=3D"Yann LeCun">Yann LeCun</a> saw a world model (defined by him as a <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_network_(machine_learning)" titl=
e=3D"Neural network (machine learning)">neural network</a> that acts as a <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_model" title=3D"Mental model=
">mental model</a> for aspects of the world that are seen as relevant) as p=
art of a larger system of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitiv=
e_architecture" title=3D"Cognitive architecture">cognitive architecture</a>=
 =E2=80=93 other neural networks that are analogous to different regions of=
 the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brain" title=3D"Brain">brain<=
/a>. In his view, this framework could lead to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Commonsense_reasoning" title=3D"Commonsense reasoning">commons=
ense reasoning</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-49" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-49"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>49<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-:3_50-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:3-50"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>50<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> LeCun has estimate=
d that world models would be fully functional by the late 2020s<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-51" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fo=
undation_model#cite_note-51"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>51<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> to mid 2030s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-5=
2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_=
model#cite_note-52"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>52<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Training">Training</h4><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp=
;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8" title=3D"Edit section: Training"><span>edit=
</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>World models are trained on a variety of data modalities, including text=
, images, audio and video, and have been applied to <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Text-to-video_model" title=3D"Text-to-video model">video =
generation</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:4_53-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:4-53"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>53<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=
 One open source dataset for world models includes 1 billion data points ac=
ross multiple modalities (text, images, audio, video and <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_clouds" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Point c=
louds">point clouds</a>), including 1 million <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Data_annotation" title=3D"Data annotation">manual annotations</=
a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:10_41-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:10-41"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>41<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Examples">Examples</h4><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp=
;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D9" title=3D"Edit section: Examples"><span>edit=
</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p><i>TechCrunch</i> saw <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sora_(tex=
t-to-video_model)" title=3D"Sora (text-to-video model)">Sora</a> as an exam=
ple of a world model,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:4_53-1" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-:4-53"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>53<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup> while in January 2025, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvid=
ia" title=3D"Nvidia">Nvidia</a> released its own set of world models.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-54" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Foundation_model#cite_note-54"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>54<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-:9_39-1" =
