<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Technical_details">Technica=
l details</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-=
bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFo=
undation_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D12" title=3D"Edit section: T=
echnical details"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracke=
t">]</span></span></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Model_architecture">Model a=
rchitecture</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsectio=
n-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3D=
Foundation_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13" title=3D"Edit section:=
 Model architecture"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bra=
cket">]</span></span></div>
<p>For a foundation model to effectively generalize, it must acquire rich r=
epresentations of the training data. As a result, expressive model architec=
tures that efficiently process large-scale data are often preferred in buil=
ding foundation models.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Bommasani_20210818_18-1" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#ci=
te_note-Bommasani_20210818-18"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>18<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Currently, the <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformer_(deep_learning_architecture)" class=3D"=
mw-redirect" title=3D"Transformer (deep learning architecture)">Transformer=
</a> architecture is the de facto choice for building foundation models acr=
oss a range of modalities.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-73" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-73"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>73<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Training_2">Training</h3><s=
pan class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&a=
mp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D14" title=3D"Edit section: Training"><span>e=
dit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Foundation models are built by optimizing a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Loss_function" title=3D"Loss function">loss function</a>, whic=
h is a mathematical function that determines how model parameters are updat=
ed based on model predictions on training data.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-74" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#c=
ite_note-74"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>74<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> Language models are often trained with a next-toke=
ns prediction objective, which refers to the extent at which the model is a=
ble to predict the next token in a sequence. Image models are commonly trai=
ned with contrastive learning or diffusion training objectives. For contras=
tive learning, images are randomly augmented before being evaluated on the =
resulting similarity of the model's representations. For diffusion models, =
images are noised and the model learns to gradually de-noise via the object=
ive. Multimodal training objectives also exist, with some separating images=
 and text during training, while others examine them concurrently.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-75" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Foundation_model#cite_note-75"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>75<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In general, the training obje=
ctives for foundation models promote the learning of broadly useful represe=
ntations of data.
</p><p>With the rise of foundation models and the larger datasets that powe=
r them, a training objective must be able to parse through internet-scale d=
ata for meaningful data points. Additionally, since foundation models are d=
esigned to solve a general range of tasks, training objectives ought to be =
<i>domain complete</i>, or able to solve a broad set of downstream capabili=
ties within the given domain. Lastly, foundation model training objectives =
should seek to scale well and be computationally efficient. With model size=
 and compute power both being relevant constraints, a training objective mu=
st be able to overcome such bottlenecks.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Data">Data</h3><span class=
=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp;actio=
n=3Dedit&amp;section=3D15" title=3D"Edit section: Data"><span>edit</span></=
a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Foundation models are trained on a large quantity of data, working under=
 the maxim "the more data, the better."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-76" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note=
-76"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>76<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> Performance evaluation does show that more data generally =
leads to better performance, but other issues arise as data quantity grows.=
 Tasks like managing the dataset, integrating data across new applications,=
 ensuring adherence to data licenses, and maintaining data quality all beco=
me more difficult as data size grows. The specific demands of foundation mo=
dels have only exacerbated such issues, as it remains the norm for large fo=
undation models to use public web-scraped data. Foundation models include a=
lso search engines data and SEO meta tags data. Public web data remains a p=
lentiful resource, but it also demands stringent moderation and data proces=
sing from foundation model developers before it can be successfully integra=
ted into the training pipeline.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-77" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-77"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>77<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup>
</p><p>Training foundation models often runs the risk of violating user pri=
vacy, as private data can be disclosed, collected, or used in ways beyond t=
he stated scope. Even if no private data is leaked, models can still inadve=
rtently compromise security through learned behavior in the resulting found=
ation model.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-78" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-78"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>78<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Data quali=
ty is another key point, as web-scraped data frequently contains biased, du=
plicate, and toxic material. Once foundation models are deployed, ensuring =
high-quality data is still an issue, as undesirable behavior can still emer=
ge from small subsets of data.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Systems">Systems</h3><span =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp;a=
ction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D16" title=3D"Edit section: Systems"><span>edit</=
span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The size of foundation models also brings about issues with the computer=
 systems they run on. The average foundation model is too large to be run w=
ithin a single accelerator's memory and the initial training process requir=
es an expensive amount of resources.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-79" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-79=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>79<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup> Such issues are predicted to further exacerbate in future as =
foundation models grow to new heights. Due to this constraint, researchers =
have begun looking into compressing model size through tight model inferenc=
e.
</p><p>GPUs are the most common choice of compute hardware for machine lear=
ning, due to high memory storage and strong power. Typical foundation model=
 training requires many GPUs in a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Distributed_computing" title=3D"Distributed computing">distributed computin=
g</a> setup, connected in parallel with fast interconnects. Acquiring a suf=
ficient amount of GPUs of requisite compute efficiency is a challenge for m=
any foundation model developers, one that has led to an increasing dilemma =
in the field. Larger models require greater compute power, but often at the=
 cost of improved compute efficiency. Since training remains time-consuming=
 and expensive, the tradeoff between compute power and compute efficiency h=
as led only a few select companies to afford the production costs for large=
, state of the art foundation models. Some techniques like compression and =
distillation can make inference more affordable, but they fail to completel=
y shore up this weakness.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Scaling">Scaling</h3><span =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model&amp;a=
ction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D17" title=3D"Edit section: Scaling"><span>edit</=
span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The accuracy and capabilities of foundation models often scale predictab=
ly with the size of the model and the amount of the training data. Specific=
ally, scaling laws have been discovered, which are data-based empirical tre=
nds that relate resources (data, model size, compute usage) to model capabi=
lities. Particularly, a model's scale is defined by compute, dataset size, =
and the number of parameters, all of which exhibit a power-law relationship=
 with end performance.
</p><p>However, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broken_Neural_Scal=
ing_Law" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Broken Neural Scaling Law">broken s=
caling laws</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-80" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-80"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>80<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> have be=
en discovered in which this relationship smoothly transitions (at points re=
ferred to as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broken_Neural_Scaling=
_Law" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Broken Neural Scaling Law">break(s)</a=
>) from a power law with one exponent to a power law with another (differen=
t) exponent. When one does not collect any points near (or after) the break=
(s), it can be difficult to obtain an accurate extrapolation.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Adaptation">Adaptation</h3>=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</sp=
an><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model=
&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D18" title=3D"Edit section: Adaptation"><sp=
an>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></d=
iv>
<p>Foundation models are inherently multi-purpose: to use these model for a=
 specific use case requires some form of adaptation. At a minimum, models n=
eed to be adapted to perform the task of interest (task specification), but=
 often better performance can be achieved by more extensive adaptation to t=
he domain of interest (domain specialization).
</p><p>A variety of methods (e.g. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Prompt_engineering" title=3D"Prompt engineering">prompting</a>, <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-context_learning" class=3D"mw-redirect" ti=
tle=3D"In-context learning">in-context learning</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Fine-tuning_(deep_learning)" title=3D"Fine-tuning (deep =
learning)">fine-tuning</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LoRA" =
class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"LoRA">LoRA</a>) provide different tradeoffs =
between the costs of adaptation and the extent to which models are speciali=
zed. Some major facets to consider when adapting a foundation model are com=
pute budget and data availability. Foundation models can be very large, up =
to trillions of parameters in size, so adapting the entirety of a foundatio=
n model can be computationally expensive. Therefore, developers sometimes a=
dapt only the last neural layer or only the bias vectors to save time and s=
pace.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-81" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-81"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>81<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> For particularly =
niche applications, specific data may also not be available to adapt the fo=
undation model sufficiently. In such circumstances, data must be manually l=
abeled, which is costly and can demand expert knowledge.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Evaluation">Evaluation</h3>=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</sp=
an><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_model=
&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D19" title=3D"Edit section: Evaluation"><sp=
an>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></d=
iv>
<p>Evaluation is a key part of developing foundation models. Not only does =
evaluation allow for tracking progress of high-performance models, it also =
creates benchmarks for future model development. Stakeholders rely on evalu=
ations to understand model behaviors and gain insight into their various at=
tributes. Traditionally, foundation models are evaluated relative to each o=
ther through standardized task benchmarks like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/MMLU" title=3D"MMLU">MMLU</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-82" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_=
note-82"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>82<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> MMMU,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-83" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-83"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>83<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> HumanEval,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-84" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-84"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>84<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and GSM8=
K.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-85" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-85"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>85<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Given that foundatio=
n models are multi-purpose, increasingly meta-benchmarks are developed that=
 aggregate different underlying benchmarks. Examples include LM-Harness,<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-86" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-86"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
86<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> BIG-Bench,<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-87" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Found=
ation_model#cite_note-87"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>87<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> HELM,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-88" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_=
note-88"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>88<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> OpenLLM Leaderboard,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-89" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_no=
te-89"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>89<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup> DecodingTrust,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-90" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-90"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>90<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> and HEIM.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-91" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-91"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>91<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Since foundation models' utility depends on their own general capabi=
lities and the performance of fine-tuned applications, evaluation must cove=
r both metrics. Proper evaluation examines both a foundation model's downst=
ream applications in aggregate and the direct properties the foundation mod=
el holds. To ensure further equity in evaluation, certain existing evaluati=
on frameworks account for all adaptation resources, which leads to more inf=
ormed analyses for the benefit of all stakeholders.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-92" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_mod=
el#cite_note-92"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>92<span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Supply_chain">Supply chain<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFoundation_m=
odel&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D20" title=3D"Edit section: Supply chai=
n"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></sp=
an></div>
<p>Foundation models' general capabilities allow them to fulfill a unique r=
ole in the AI ecosystem,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-93" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-93"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>93<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> fueled by many upstream and downstream technologies.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-:=
1_1-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundat=
ion_model#cite_note-:1-1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Training a foundation model requires s=
everal resources (e.g. data, compute, labor, hardware, code), with foundati=
on models often involving immense amounts of data and compute (also referre=
d to as computational power). Due to foundation models' large development c=
osts and inexpensive adaptation requirements, the AI landscape has shifted =
to a small subset of AI companies making foundation models for downstream a=
daptation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-94" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-94"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">[</span>94<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Thus, most f=
oundation model companies outsource this step to specialized data providers=
 (e.g. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scale_AI" title=3D"Scale AI=
">Scale AI</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-95" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-95"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>95<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surge_AI" title=3D"Surge AI">Surge</a><su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-96" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-96"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
96<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>) and compute providers (e=
.g. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_Bedrock" title=3D"Amazo=
n Bedrock">Amazon Bedrock</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goo=
gle_Cloud" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Google Cloud">Google Cloud</a>, <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_Azure" title=3D"Microsoft=
 Azure">Microsoft Azure</a>).
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Estimated_training_cost_of_some_AI_models_-_2=
024_AI_index.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.=
wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Estimated_training_cost_of_some_=
AI_models_-_2024_AI_index.jpg/500px-Estimated_training_cost_of_some_AI_mode=
ls_-_2024_AI_index.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"430" height=3D"229" sty=
le=3D"--mw-file-upright: 1.7" class=3D"mw-file-element mw-file-upright" dat=
a-file-width=3D"2560" data-file-height=3D"1361"></a><figcaption>Investment =
in computing capabilities to train larger AI models has rapidly increased.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-97" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-97"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>97<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p>The foundation model developer itself will then take the data and use th=
e supplied compute to actually train the foundation model. After the founda=
tion model is completely built, much of the data and labor requirements aba=
te. In this development process, hardware and compute are the most necessar=
y, and also the most exclusive resources. To train larger and more complex =
AI, a sufficient amount of compute is key. However, compute is consolidated=
 in the hands of a few, select entities, which most foundation model develo=
pers depend on. As such, the foundation model pipeline is concentrated heav=
ily around these providers. Compute is also costly; in 2023, AI companies s=
pent more than 80% of total capital on compute resources.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-98" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundati=
on_model#cite_note-98"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>98<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Foundation models require a large amount of general data to power th=
eir capabilities. Early foundation models scraped from subsets of the inter=
net to provide this data information. As the size and scope of foundation m=
odels grows, larger quantities of internet scraping becomes necessary, resu=
lting in higher likelihoods of biased or toxic data. This toxic or biased d=
ata can disproportionately harm marginalized groups and exacerbate existing=
 prejudices.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-99" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-99"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>99<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>To address this issue of low-quality data that arose with unsupervis=
ed training, some foundation model developers have turned to manual filteri=
ng. This practice, known as data labor, comes with its own host of issues.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-100" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-100"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>100<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Such manual data det=
oxification is often outsourced to reduce labor costs, with some workers ma=
king less than $2 per hour.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-101" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-101"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>101<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup>
</p><p>The foundation model will then be hosted online either via the devel=
oper or via an external organization. Once released, other parties can crea=
te applications based on the foundation model, whether through fine-tuning =
or wholly new purposes. People can then access these applications to serve =
their various means, allowing one foundation model to power and reach a wid=
e audience.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Release_strategies">Release=
 strategies</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsectio=
n-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3D=
Foundation_model&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D21" title=3D"Edit section:=
 Release strategies"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bra=
cket">]</span></span></div>
<p>After a foundation model is built, it can be released in one of many way=
s. There are many facets to a release: the asset itself, who has access, ho=
w access changes over time, and the conditions on use.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-1=
02" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation=
_model#cite_note-102"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>102<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> All these factors contribute to how a =
foundation model will affect downstream applications.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-10=
3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_=
model#cite_note-103"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>103<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In particular, the two most common forms =
of foundation model release are through APIs and direct model downloads.
</p><p>When a model is released via an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/API" title=3D"API">API</a>, users can query the model and receive resp=
onses, but cannot directly access the model itself. Comparatively, the mode=
l could be directly downloadable for users to access and modify. Both relea=
se strategies are often classified as an open release. The exact definition=
 of an open release is disputed, but widely accepted requirements are provi=
ded by the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Source_Initiative"=
 title=3D"Open Source Initiative">Open Source Initiative</a>.
</p><p>Some open foundation models are: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/PaLM" title=3D"PaLM">PaLM 2</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/LLaMA" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"LLaMA">Llama 2</a>, <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_Granite" title=3D"IBM Granite">Granite</a>=
, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mistral_AI" title=3D"Mistral=
 AI">Mistral</a>. While open foundation models can further research and dev=
elopment more easily, they are also more susceptible to misuse. Open founda=
tion models can be downloaded by anyone, and particularly powerful models c=
an be fine-tuned to intentionally or unintentionally cause harm.<sup class=
=3D"noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact" style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<=
i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title=
=3D"Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span title=3D"This claim needs references t=
o reliable sources. (July 2025)">citation needed</span></a></i>]</sup>
</p><p>During a closed release, the foundation model cannot be accessed by =
the public, but is used internally by an organization. Such releases are co=
nsidered safer, but offer no additional value to the research community or =
the public at large.
</p><p>Some foundation models like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Google_DeepMind" title=3D"Google DeepMind">Google DeepMind</a>'s Flamingo<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-104" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Foundation_model#cite_note-104"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>104<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> are fully closed, me=
aning they are available only to the model developer; others, such as <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI" title=3D"OpenAI">OpenAI</a>'s <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPT-4" title=3D"GPT-4">GPT-4</a>, a=
re limited access, available to the public but only as a <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_box#Science_and_technology" title=3D"Black box=
">black box</a>; and still others, such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Meta_Platforms" title=3D"Meta Platforms">Meta</a>'s Llama 2 are op=
en, with broadly available model weights enabling downstream modification a=
nd scrutiny.
</p>
